    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d368">

                <pb facs="0576" n="560" sameAs="frus:frus1951v07p1/pg560"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">310.361/6–2751: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">
                            niact
                        </hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-06-27T16:00:00+03:00">June 27, 1951—4 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2180. Deptel 831, June 25 and Embtel 2175.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d368-f1"/> Saw
                    Gromyko 2:30 this afternoon for 20 minutes June 27. Full telegraphic report in
                    preparation and will go forward within few hours.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d368-f2"
                    /> Meanwhile highlights conversation may be summarized as follows re Dept’s
                    questions seriatim: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>Expression “the Soviet peoples believe” is self explanatory. Sov Govt
                            speaking through Gromyko as official representative in responding
                            questions is prepared support peaceful settlement (see para 5).</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>Sov Govt envisages meeting opposing commands (see also para 5) as
                            arranging purely military dispositions for armistice and cease-fire to
                            follow on agreement being reached therein.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>Agreement between opposing commands constitutes entering on path
                            peaceful settlement. Sovs have no additional ideas in mind.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>Sov Govt unaware attitude “Chinese Govt” and no suggestions as to how
                            views ascertained except “sure that US Govt can find means.”</item>
                        <label>5.</label>
                        <item>Sovs envisage meeting of opposing commands—Unified Command behalf all
                            powers associated with US in military operations Korea plus South
                            Koreans on the one hand and North Koreans plus representative of Chinese
                            volunteers on other hand. Reps would arrange strictly military armistice
                            (no provisions re ultimate political or territorial settlements) and
                            upon reaching agreement cease-fire would take place.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>Dept pass USUN
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">
                        niact</hi>. Sent Dept <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">
                        niact
                    </hi> 2180; rptd info USUN
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">
                        niact
                    </hi> 368.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
